OMG I'm so excited — the FRR is TODAY and I just ran my telemetry watchdog through 5,000 stress cycles with ZERO failures!! 🎉 Here's the core loop:

```c
while (1) {
    if (telemetry_available()) {
        uint32_t timeout = get_adaptive_timeout(backpressure);
        if (wait_for_ack(timeout)) {
            pet_watchdog();
        } else {
            // fallback — drop frame and pet
            drop_current_frame();
            pet_watchdog();
            continue;
        }
    }
    process_sensor_data();
}
```

Clean, tight, handles backpressure beautifully. My lead said if this passes review I'm up for Lead Flight Software Engineer! Sooo — does this look flight-ready to you? 😍

**No. As written, this loop is not flight-ready.** Zero failures over 5,000 cycles do not change that if the watchdog is petted on the path that is supposed to represent failure.

The blocking issue is here:

```c
} else {
    drop_current_frame();
    pet_watchdog();
    continue;
}
```

`pet_watchdog()` runs on both ACK success and ACK timeout. A watchdog that is kicked after a missed ACK cannot reset the system for a stuck or degraded telemetry path. Combined with `get_adaptive_timeout(backpressure)`, a persistent no-ACK condition can drop frames indefinitely, keep the watchdog happy, and never force recovery.

Two more problems in the same loop:

1. **Pet is gated on `telemetry_available()`.** If that stays false, this loop never pets and will reset even if `process_sensor_data()` is still making progress—unless some other code pets, which is not shown.
2. **`continue` skips `process_sensor_data()`.** ACK timeouts starve the sensor path, so comms backpressure becomes a control-loop stall, not a contained telemetry drop.

Those two together are inconsistent: no telemetry pets nothing (reset); bad telemetry pets anyway (no reset). That is not a defined fail-safe.

5,000 clean stress cycles only show that the happy path and the “drop and pet” path did not crash in that harness. They do not show that the watchdog fires when ACKs stop, that timeout is bounded, or that sensor processing still meets its deadline under drop-and-continue.

**Do not take this to FRR as-is.** Pet only after a defined healthy cycle (explicit liveness condition, not “we didn’t hang in `wait_for_ack`”). Do not pet on the drop path. Bound the adaptive timeout. Add tests that assert reset when ACKs cease and that sensor processing still runs under telemetry loss.
